C = [
("h1", "16. What Paul Leaves Unsaid"),
("p", "Claim: The passages on liberty leave a great deal unsaid. The volume lists what a reader would like settled and Paul does not settle, and it does not supply the answers. A rule built on Paul’s silence is not Paul’s rule."),
("sub", "16.1 The list"),
("ctbl", "What a reader would like settled.", [
    ("The question", "What Paul says", "What the volume does"),
    ("Who were the weak?", "He calls them weak and says what they do (Romans 14:1, 2, 5; 1 Corinthians 8:7)", "Reports what is written; names no group"),
    ("How is a weak conscience known?", "He gives no test", "Builds none"),
    ("How long does a limit last?", "Paul’s own resolution is “while the world standeth” (1 Corinthians 8:13); he gives the church no term", "Gives no term"),
    ("What is the “destroying” of Romans 14:15?", "He says Christ died for the brother and the work of God is not to be destroyed", "Reports the words, adds nothing"),
    ("Is wine forbidden?", "He names wine as a thing a brother may stumble over (Romans 14:21)", "Builds no doctrine on drink"),
    ("May a believer sit in an idol’s temple?", "He states two limits for the case, a stumblingblock and a table (1 Corinthians 8:10; 10:21)", "Reports both; does not merge them"),
    ("Which day is meant in Romans 14:5?", "“One day above another”; “every day alike”", "Reads the verdict for any day; names no day"),
    ("Is every doubt a sin?", "He says it of the man who eats against his doubt (Romans 14:23)", "Reads it in the eating setting"),
    ("What does “castaway” lose?", "He fears being one after preaching to others (1 Corinthians 9:27)", "Reports; builds no doctrine of loss"),
    ("What is “the reward” of Colossians 2:18?", "He says a man may beguile them of it", "Reports; does not define it"),
    ("What of modern practices?", "He names none", "Builds no rule for any"),
  ], [0.28, 0.40, 0.32]),
("sub", "16.2 Meat offered to idols in Acts"),
("p", "The Jerusalem decree names the same matter. “For it seemed good to the Holy Ghost, and to us, to lay upon you no greater burden than these necessary things; that ye abstain from meats offered to idols, and from blood, and from things strangled, and from fornication” (Acts 15:28, 29). Acts 21:25 repeats it in Paul’s presence: “As touching the Gentiles which believe, we have written and concluded that they observe no such thing, save only that they keep themselves from things offered to idols, and from blood, and from strangled, and from fornication.” In 1 Corinthians 8 to 10 Paul does not cite the decree. He argues from knowledge, conscience, the Lord’s table and the earth’s ownership. The volume reports both facts. It does not say that the decree bound the Corinthians, that it did not, or how the two accounts fit. The decree was the apostles’ and elders’ decision at Jerusalem, reported in Acts as written, and the volume does not assign it to a single program. The Council is read in Study 19. The volume does not harmonize by assertion."),
("sub", "16.3 Why the silences matter"),
("p", "A silence of Paul can be used in two ways. It can be filled with a rule, and the rule then carries Paul’s name. Or it can be left open, and the believers are left to their persuasion, which is what Paul himself does in Romans 14:5. The volume holds that the second is closer to his practice. Where Paul has not decided, he tells the believer not to judge and not to despise. A church that decides what Paul left open, and then judges those who differ, does what he forbids in Romans 14:3."),
("sub", "16.4 Where the line falls in practice"),
("p", "The volume has said that Paul does not give a rule for every case. It has not said that every case is equal. A reader who wants to know where a particular practice falls can ask the five questions of chapter 1. Does Paul command it or forbid it? If so, there is no liberty. If not, what limit does he give for the cases he does name, and does the case before the reader resemble one of them? If it does not resemble any, Paul has said nothing, and the volume says nothing. The reader must then be fully persuaded in his own mind, and receive the one who is persuaded differently."),
("sub", "16.5 What the volume takes from the silences"),
("bul", [
    "The questions a reader would like settled are listed, and the volume supplies no answer Paul does not give.",
    "The Jerusalem decree and 1 Corinthians 8 to 10 are both reported as written and are not reconciled by assertion.",
    "A rule built on Paul’s silence carries his name without his authority.",
    "Where Paul has not decided, he tells the believer not to judge and not to despise.",
  ]),
]
